We investigate key properties of several types of Gelfand-Shilov spaces, including how they relate to each other, the growth of their polynomial bases, and connections to spaces that are invariant under the Fourier transform. The conditions under which these spaces are nontrivial are also derived. The analysis includes the examination of the space's behavior with respect to orthogonal and entire functions, as well as its relation to the Hermite function and its implications for the non-occurrence of the Riemann Hypothesis for certain values. The Gelfand-Shilov spaces are defined by the following inequality:

\begin{equation}
|{\text{f}}(x)|\leq C_{\text{f}}{\text{e}}^{\varkappa |x|^{\sigma}},
\end{equation}

where the absolute value of a function is bounded by an exponential expression involving the function's input.
